{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 7, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a prominent Crusader noble who ruled as the Count of Tripoli from around 1127 until his death in 1152. He belonged to the House of Toulouse and was the son of Ponce Count of Tripoli and Cecilia of France. Raymond’s reign was marked by significant political maneuvering and military engagement in the Levant during the height of the Crusader states.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n**Early Life and Succession**\nRaymond III inherited the County of Tripoli following the death of his uncle Baldwin II who was also King of Jerusalem. Baldwin had been acting as regent for Raymond since 1112 when Raymond’s father Ponce died. Baldwin’s elevation to the kingship of Jerusalem in 1118 left Raymond under the guardianship of William of Bures until he came of age. Raymond officially took control of Tripoli around 1127 but faced challenges in consolidating power immediately due to internal rivalries and external threats.\n\nDuring his early years Raymond engaged in conflicts with local Muslim rulers particularly with the Artuqid dynasty. In 1129 he participated in the Second Crusade which was launched to recapture the city of Damascus. Though the crusade ultimately failed Raymond’s involvement highlighted his commitment to the broader Crusader cause.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n**Political Struggles and Alliances**\nRaymond’s rule was complicated by the complex political landscape of the Crusader states. He maintained alliances with other Crusader leaders such as Joscelin II Count of Edessa and Fulk of Anjou King of Jerusalem. His relationship with King Fulk was particularly important as they collaborated in several military campaigns against Muslim forces including the Zengid dynasty led by Zengi.\n\nOne of Raymond’s notable achievements was his role in the defense of the Crusader states against the growing threat posed by Nur ad-Din a powerful Zengid leader. Raymond participated in the Battle of Inab in 1149 where Crusader forces suffered a significant defeat against Nur ad-Din’s army. This battle underscored the increasing military prowess of the Zengids and the need for greater unity among the Crusader states.\n\nIn addition to his military engagements Raymond was involved in diplomatic efforts to secure the safety of the Crusader states. He engaged in negotiations with various Muslim leaders attempting to form alliances and secure truces although these efforts often proved temporary due to the shifting dynamics of power in the region.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n**Marriage and Legacy**\nRaymond III married Hodierna of Tripoli the daughter of Tancred Prince of Galilee in 1129. Their marriage produced three children Raymond IV Raymond V and Alice who became the next Countess of Tripoli upon Raymond III’s death in 1152. Hodierna played a significant role in the governance of Tripoli and continued Raymond’s efforts to defend against Muslim invasions.\n\nRaymond III died in 1152 and was succeeded by his eldest son Raymond IV who was still a minor at the time of his father’s death. Hodierna acted as regent during Raymond IV’s minority and later married Reynald of Châtillon a controversial figure known for his aggressive policies against Muslim states. Raymond III’s reign is remembered for his contributions to the defense of the Crusader states his diplomatic endeavors and his efforts to maintain the independence of Tripoli amidst the turbulent political environment of the 12th century. His legacy endures as a symbol of resistance and adaptability in the face of overwhelming challenges.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.08366527408361435}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.0001662831346038729}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.005333361681550741}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.24919773638248444}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00035089056473225355}, {"token": " Trip